target: internal/record/record.go
verify: go test ./internal/push/ -count=1 -run 'TestCheckRefusesAnUnreadableRecord'
verify: go test ./cmd/looper/ -count=1 -run 'TestPrReportRefusesARunItCannotRead'
expect: --- FAIL:
why:    RETARGETED. The predicate moved from internal/push into record.Run.Empty when the report slice needed the same answer, so the guard follows the definition. `r == nil` alone is the narrower, wrong test: a row carrying the id under a phase the typed decode does not own — `{"run_id":"x","phase":"nudge"}` — creates a *Run that says nothing, and the map lookup succeeds. From that the convergence derives `unknown` (a DISCLOSURE, not a block), no recorded outcome can block, and the report's fingerprint collapses to `0 cycle(s) · 0 agent(s)` — exactly what push-check requires, so a GENUINE report off nothing satisfies the gate with no forgery. It shipped in that narrower form once and PR #4's review caught it; the report slice then shipped the same narrowing in `looper pr-report`, measured. Two verify commands because both halves of the gate now read this one definition, which is the point of moving it.
--- old
	return r == nil || (r.Plan == nil && len(r.Cycles) == 0 && r.Finish == nil)
--- new
	return r == nil
